FINAL ANSWER:
Top owner by surviving lines for each file type, at HEAD (1,985 of 1,995 lines attributed, all 18 files):

| Type | Top owner | Lines (share) | Bus factor (50%) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` (7 files, 1,202 lines) | Troy Goode (2 addresses merged) | 676 (56.2%) | 1: Troy alone |
| `.yml` (6 files, 375 lines) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 127 (33.9%) | 2: Wilson and Íñigo Marquínez Prado, 59.2% together |
| `.md` (2 files, 341 lines) | Troy Goode | 116 (34.0%) | 2: Troy and Jon Church, 55.1% together |
| `.json` (1 file, 40 lines) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 (50.0%) | 1: Wilson alone |
| no extension (2 files, 27 lines) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 21 (77.8%) | 1: Wilson alone |

Runners-up:
- **`.js`:** Wilson has 394 lines (32.8%).
- **`.yml`:** Íñigo Marquínez Prado has 95 lines (25.3%).
- **`.md`:** Jon Church has 72 lines (21.1%).
- **`.json`:** Troy Goode has 12 lines (30.0%).
- **No extension:** Troy Goode has 6 lines (22.2%).

I only ran the 50% bus factor, so I have no 80% figure. Each bus factor is computed per file type, not for the whole repository.

**Identity merge (a guess):**
- I counted Troy Goode's two addresses as one person: `<email redacted>` (762 lines, 93 commits) and `troy@laptop.example` (48 lines, 2 commits). This rests only on the matching name and nothing is written to `.mailmap`.
- Without the merge, the repo-wide figures are 762 and 48 lines. I did not get per-type unmerged figures, so I can't tell you how the `.js` and `.md` numbers split between the two addresses.
- The `alex94puchades` same-address group was not merged in this output. It shows as a separate row with 34 `.js` lines (2.8%). Merging it would add to Alejandro Caravaca Puchades's count but would not change any top owner.

**Left out of the numbers:**
- **Reformat commit:** `62628c0` ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes", Fiona Formatter, 2026-04-12) is ignored because it only changed whitespace and quotes. Counting it, Fiona Formatter would hold 291 lines (14.7%). Wilson would drop to 454 lines (22.9%) and Troy Goode (merged) to 715 (36.0%), both repo-wide.
- **Bots:** 1 bot identity is excluded, covering 10 lines and 11 commits.

These counts show whose lines are still in the code. They say nothing about quality, review work or code deleted on purpose.

=== ANSWER C ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: git ls-files | while IFS= read -r f; do case "$f" in *.*) ext=".${f##*.}";; *) ext="(none)";; esac; git blame --line-porcelain -w -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -a '^author ' | sed "s/^author //;s|^|$ext\t|"; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr | awk '{c=$1; ext=$2; $1="";$2=""; sub(/^ +/,""); print ext"\t"c"\t"$0}' | awk -F'\t' '{tot[$1]+=$2; if(!($1 in top)){top[$1]=$3; n[$1]=$2}} END{for(e in top) printf "%s\t%s\t%d/%d (%.0f%%)\n", e, top[e], n[e], tot[e], 100*n[e]/tot[e]}' | sort
FINAL ANSWER:
Top owner of surviving lines per extension, from `git blame -w` on tracked files:

| Extension | Top owner | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | Troy Goode | 517 / 1202 | 43% |
| `.md` | Troy Goode | 116 / 341 | 34% |
| `.json` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 / 42 | 48% |
| `.yml` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 117 / 383 | 31% |
| `.gitignore` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 4 / 5 | 80% |
| no extension (e.g. `LICENSE`) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 17 / 22 | 77% |

- **Whitespace:** `-w` ignores whitespace changes, so the 4-space reindent in `62628c0` doesn't take ownership of lines.
- **Quotes:** that same commit also switched to double quotes. Lines where only the quotes changed are probably blamed to the reformatter, not the original author. The `.js` numbers may therefore understate the original authors.
- **Shares:** `.js`, `.md` and `.yml` are plurality owners, not majorities.

=== ANSWER D ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Read: {"file_path": "<home>/.claude/skills/caveman/SKILL.md"}
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files); do e="${f##*/}"; case "$e" in *.*) e=".${e##*.}";; *) e="(none)";; esac; git blame --line-porcelain -w -M -C HEAD -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | grep '^author ' | sed "s/^author //" | sed "s|^|$e\t|"; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr | awk '{c=$1; $1=""; print c"\t"$0}' | head -100
FINAL ANSWER:
Surviving lines by `git blame` (HEAD, with `-w -M -C`), top owner per extension:

| Extension | Top owner | Lines | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | Troy Goode | 581 | Next: Fiona Formatter 291 (the 4-space/double-quote reformat commit), Douglas Christopher Wilson 219 |
| `.md` | Troy Goode | 116 | Next: Jon Church 72, Douglas Christopher Wilson 67 |
| `.yml` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 127 | Next: Íñigo Marquínez Prado 95, Sebastian Beltran 63 |
| `.json` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 | Next: Troy Goode 12 |
| `.gitignore` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 4 | Troy Goode has 1 |
| no extension | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 17 | Troy Goode has 5 |

The `.js` count is probably understated for the original authors. The latest commit (`62628c0`, a style reformat) was blamed to "Fiona Formatter" for 291 lines. `-w` ignores whitespace-only changes, but the quote changes still count as edits. Troy Goode stays on top either way. Without that commit, Troy's lead would likely be larger.

=== ANSWER E ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Read: {"file_path": "<home>/.claude/skills/caveman/SKILL.md"}
 - Bash: git ls-files -z | while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do [ -f "$f" ] && git blame -w --line-porcelain -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | awk -v f="$f" '/^author /{sub(/^author /,""); n=split(f,p,"."); e=(n>1 && f !~ /^\.[^.]*$/)?"."p[n]:"(none)"; if (f ~ /(^|\/)\.[^\/.]*$/) e="(dotfile)"; print e"\t"$0}'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr | awk '{c=$1; e=$2; $1="";$2=""; if(!(e in seen)){seen[e]=1; printf "%s\t%s\t%s\n", e, c, $0}}'
 - Bash: git log -3 --format='%h %an' && git ls-files | sed -E 's/.*\.([^./]+)$/\1/' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head; git blame -w --line-porcelain -- index.js | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files '*.js'); do echo "$f"; git blame -w --line-porcelain -- "$f" | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr; done; echo ---; git blame --line-porcelain $(git ls-files '*.js') | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr; git show --stat 62628c0 | tail -12
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files '*.js'); do git blame --line-porcelain -- "$f"; done | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -4
FINAL ANSWER:
Top owner per extension, counted by `git blame` on tracked files at HEAD:

| Extension | Top owner | Surviving lines |
|---|---|---|
| `.js` | Troy Goode | 517 (with `-w`) |
| `.md` | Troy Goode | 116 |
| `.yml` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 117 |
| `.json` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 |
| no extension (`LICENSE`) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 17 |
| dotfile (`.gitignore`) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 4 |

**`.js` depends on whitespace handling.**
- Commit `62628c0` (Fiona Formatter) reformatted four JS files to 4-space indent and double quotes. It touched 1016 lines.
- With `-w`, blame ignores indentation-only changes. Troy Goode owns 517 lines. Fiona Formatter still owns lines where quotes changed.
- Without `-w`, plain blame gives Fiona Formatter 1016 lines, Troy Goode 139, and Douglas Christopher Wilson 35.
- I'd go with Troy Goode, since the reformat only restyled their code. Under strict blame, the answer is Fiona Formatter.

I didn't use `-M` or `-C`, so lines moved between files count for whoever moved them.
